              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Relationships**\n\nI'm focusing on the grid's initial setup: the 10x10 structure, and the placement of the colored 2x2 squares. I've noted the specifics from the training example, particularly the positions of the blue (1) and red (2) squares. My initial observation is about the output and this example. It looks like the blue (1) square extends diagonally up-left. This seems to be the key.\n\n\n**Investigating Movement Patterns**\n\nOkay, I've got a much better handle on the movement. It seems the direction a square \"shoots\" is relative to the other. I've been dissecting the second example, noting how the diagonals of 1s and 2s emerge. I think I'm seeing a connection between their initial relative positions (above/below and left/right) and their directional trajectories, which seem to be *away* from each other. The slope (+1, +1) seems key.\n\n\n**Discovering Parallel Lines**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I noticed the second example; the lines form diagonals. Both the 1s and 2s shoot diagonally, but why is this? I've confirmed that the parallel line direction continues to the other examples. Each cell's row-col is the value on the diagonal!\n\n\n**Determining Edge Behavior**\n\nI've been drilling down on the edge cases and how the diagonals behave. It turns out that a number's starting point matters. The important values are r - c; the diagonals shoot out from a corner (depending on the number) to the grid edge. From the top-left corner, it's r - c.\n\n\n**Confirming Line Propagation**\n\nI've just validated a major breakthrough! The color '1' always generates an up-left diagonal, starting from its top-left corner, while color '2' consistently produces a down-right diagonal from its bottom-right corner. I've verified this across all examples, and it seems rock solid. I've performed extensive validation on Example 1 and Example 2, and Example 3 has also been verified. Now I must see if there are any exceptions.\n\n\n",
